#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61a51bf9-eac8-5fda-8030-dbec275d8750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718204} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbc903b0-38ec-5d75-a0e4-ac3d78e2ce7e" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0b3bb74f-813d-5f79-b1af-c5ea83614e71" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/97/97e7f0082e187d8db6adee4a2f68972507d62389b6dc9ab5d306afce942cf0fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=\\left(- \\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $12$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/70/700d51dc84b25655363314549c35259857bd23a162526de15cd18d67e258456d.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=6\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-6\\le t\\le 6$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $- \\frac{1}{2}(6)=-3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-6}^{6}(-3)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(-3)(6-(-6))\\\\&=-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(-36)=-144.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b9874ba-57b1-50cd-9ab7-7318e9dc82ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718207} :answer-field/id #uuid "188b47b6-bc89-5b4a-9d9d-4775d11c8b03" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4de48e01-f5c2-500a-bd05-1af2b3ac8acb" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/97/97e7f0082e187d8db6adee4a2f68972507d62389b6dc9ab5d306afce942cf0fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(- x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/33/33b0d69e4eff448684f5fdabcc31ba2f48e183917878dc9ab72c3cb51e59584b.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=1\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le 1$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $-1(1)=-1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(-1)(1-(-1))\\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(-2)=-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1892d0eb-bf50-59fa-8d4c-e7500aea7b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718201} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab462a70-808d-5ddc-bb4a-6b360519a3e5" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7aecaa91-ed41-5803-804b-b137bba2a02a" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/a3/a3872da890615e96a775dd9d5245daf571783c7cdb0e20172b78025dc7744b49.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(4 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(4 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $4(2)=8$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(8)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(8)(2-(-2))\\\\&=32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(32)=128.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63bed3f3-6e4d-5a0d-8f7b-d46e293abb6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ac80df-9399-5712-a0d5-1d288d95f2ff" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "819fa083-e186-53d5-890d-15a3188fb2f2" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/a3/a3872da890615e96a775dd9d5245daf571783c7cdb0e20172b78025dc7744b49.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(3 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(3 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $3(2)=6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(6)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(6)(2-(-2))\\\\&=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(24)=96.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee7b22c-d3c4-5794-8dfd-f459861766e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718202} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e21db4-d511-5250-8dac-cd5b76348478" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c26dfbcb-95d8-5085-9c0b-f25978d62db9" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/a3/a3872da890615e96a775dd9d5245daf571783c7cdb0e20172b78025dc7744b49.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/33/33b0d69e4eff448684f5fdabcc31ba2f48e183917878dc9ab72c3cb51e59584b.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=1\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le 1$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $1(1)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-1}^{1}(1)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(1)(1-(-1))\\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(2)=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12dcdd4b-243b-5262-a0ce-4d2e115f69fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x + y^{2} - 2 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718238} {:answer/id #uuid "2bbc9433-1a42-59d8-b477-3308f76eec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x + y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718243} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b5ac4-4a8b-58e0-8f79-987feee71d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718244} {:answer/id #uuid "730d6861-840c-5cd7-b525-d178889c61dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718252} {:answer/id #uuid "aa87649e-de81-5f23-b61d-f1a526b680b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x - y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718252} :answer-field/id #uuid "813ea694-8a51-5de3-b962-a605653182ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "374d2f11-3ed7-5b00-a5eb-f0e251b361d7" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- 2 x + y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the bottom side $C_4$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_4}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the bottom side, outward points down, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf j.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(- 2 x + y^{2})(0)+(2 y)(-1)\\\\\n&=- 2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $- 2 y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d42bcb3-12a0-5eb3-b73f-59939c102296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x y - 3 x - 2 y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718255} {:answer/id #uuid "aced24b4-d839-5b18-a1a6-fb0c5c236d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x y + 2 y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718262} {:answer/id #uuid "b997af9f-2aa5-5220-8001-e50e9a3068b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x y + 3 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718263} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d2550b-dd44-58e8-9294-59fa0511f5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- x y - 2 y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718264} {:answer/id #uuid "d07c420e-4f15-5d87-8c77-5d078ac03abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x y - 3 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718265} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bdb7708-023e-57e3-a756-be6431431cd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b5902aa-7ca9-5543-8754-ec74e58b2ded" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- x y - 2 y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x y - 3 x\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the top side $C_2$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the top side, outward points up, so $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf j.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(- x y - 2 y^{2})(0)+(2 x y - 3 x)(1)\\\\\n&=2 x y - 3 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $2 x y - 3 x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2357d91f-8906-5257-a7e9-56c6c5811d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 x y^{2} + 2 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718242} {:answer/id #uuid "3d895966-9f4a-5d20-94f8-e3a492fc886a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718248} {:answer/id #uuid "ac361965-c2fc-54b7-8d63-9442253d9392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- x y^{2} - 2 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718261} {:answer/id #uuid "f928c3ad-30a4-512b-b229-8dd81628f3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718269} {:answer/id #uuid "f9365896-e1d0-5460-a2d2-b404c5985be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x y^{2} + 2 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718248} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d2af4d0-5cc8-56b5-bf2f-61d79730f9b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a0497611-ea73-5b74-b27d-79561c79515f" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(2 x y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(x y^{2} + 2 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the right side $C_1$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the right side, outward points right, so $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf i.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(2 x y^{2})(1)+(x y^{2} + 2 y)(0)\\\\\n&=2 x y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $2 x y^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22527823-d045-5b4c-84af-bc8033be3329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- x + y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718241} {:answer/id #uuid "2f941d8d-3e4c-538f-993f-5d3e394eb415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718245} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6d6313-390c-5c72-8391-0c2da30da92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718250} {:answer/id #uuid "617601af-ce91-549c-969c-037be841d7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718251} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e8387a-510f-55dd-aec4-1021c10c6ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- x - y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718268} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1a9c5c-5567-5ecf-a8fa-1703dc7228e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a200749a-76e5-5c85-a95a-3039b7c0c871" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(x + y\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the left side $C_3$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the left side, outward points left, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf i.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(x + y)(-1)+(2 x)(0)\\\\\n&=- x - y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $- x - y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f4793cf-56ba-5408-9b8b-8898cc2d4e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x^{2} y - 2 x - 3 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718240} {:answer/id #uuid "3a527b6b-061c-5e4b-8a36-02b90ba6fc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718247} {:answer/id #uuid "48b0245c-5c31-5b4b-8ecb-42bb350846ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 x^{2} y - 3 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718249} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d75ca2-eb4d-5785-a228-484d8a31d490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718266} {:answer/id #uuid "fffc9cfe-6616-5460-97fb-c58707d91314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x^{2} y + 3 y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718266} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ea3164-bfc6-51ea-96f4-b4a788e89651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db6f9a89-9412-53b0-a3a3-d44fd491731b" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- 2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x^{2} y + 3 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the left side $C_3$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the left side, outward points left, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf i.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(- 2 x)(-1)+(2 x^{2} y + 3 y)(0)\\\\\n&=2 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $2 x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072359cc-2a43-5224-8882-767bc685a914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(3 y,\\ - x^{2} - 2 x,\\\\&\\quad x^{2} + 2 x,\\ - 3 y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718275} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf365a5-5c56-51e3-909a-d938af843285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(x^{2} + 2 x,\\ - 3 y,\\\\&\\quad 3 y,\\ - x^{2} - 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718280} {:answer/id #uuid "51f7ada3-34ef-5894-8371-376de0a15aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x^{2} - 2 x,\\ 3 y,\\\\&\\quad - 3 y,\\ x^{2} + 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718283} {:answer/id #uuid "71598b04-8d40-56ab-9d41-3a0bb7a3088e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(3 y,\\ x^{2} + 2 x,\\\\&\\quad - x^{2} - 2 x,\\ - 3 y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718287} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa5cde8-4d24-5297-9f40-2fb13fdd561d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(x^{2} + 2 x,\\ 3 y,\\\\&\\quad - 3 y,\\ - x^{2} - 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718280} :answer-field/id #uuid "558195b7-e83d-50b6-922b-94b2c91c0d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0ef86fb0-d349-5829-a3f5-10ff053a05d8" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- x^{2} - 2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(- 3 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_3}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_4}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_1}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=x^{2} + 2 x\\qquad\\text{(left side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=- 3 y\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=3 y\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=- x^{2} - 2 x\\qquad\\text{(right side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(x^{2} + 2 x,\\ - 3 y,\\\\&\\quad 3 y,\\ - x^{2} - 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cad1e3b-20b6-5879-b963-a92d5badbf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x y,\\ x y,\\\\&\\quad - 2 x,\\ 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718278} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5516d4-a79e-56b2-9582-f68e921036e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x y,\\ x y,\\\\&\\quad 2 x,\\ - 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718282} {:answer/id #uuid "63aaf770-d146-5d1b-bc51-e220e08d7737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(x y,\\ - x y,\\\\&\\quad 2 x,\\ - 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718286} {:answer/id #uuid "80d21b5d-4e31-58a9-a3c6-31ac2e3eaef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(2 x,\\ - 2 x,\\\\&\\quad - x y,\\ x y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718289} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc6a852-4595-5071-a8e7-a047dfa6ead0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- 2 x,\\ 2 x,\\\\&\\quad x y,\\ - x y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718278} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc8fc836-2edf-5952-bab7-23d23116a76b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "60bd95da-3b3c-5d80-b70a-8427042bf672" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(x y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_4}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_1}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=- x y\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=x y\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=- 2 x\\qquad\\text{(left side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=2 x\\qquad\\text{(right side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x y,\\ x y,\\\\&\\quad - 2 x,\\ 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2536ac43-622b-5d31-ae35-815cd7e018c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(2 x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad - 3 y,\\ 3 y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718279} {:answer/id #uuid "425aafef-dd4e-590a-9670-343bea434f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(2 x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad 3 y,\\ - 3 y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718281} {:answer/id #uuid "c032148f-8071-5ebf-9cf7-f72e63772880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(3 y,\\ - 3 y,\\\\&\\quad - 2 x y^{2},\\ 2 x y^{2}\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718296} {:answer/id #uuid "cccf6844-abda-5cf9-8045-b7a0e7938d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(3 y,\\ - 3 y,\\\\&\\quad 2 x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2}\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718299} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc207c9-9f74-5f24-92b8-358b174702cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- 2 x y^{2},\\ 2 x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad - 3 y,\\ 3 y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718281} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d59205-65b1-579d-9d7d-60169de6a4e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "753a525a-94cc-5d7a-8cc0-fe74cf9a5327" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(3 y\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(- 2 x y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_4}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_1}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_3}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=2 x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=- 2 x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=3 y\\qquad\\text{(right side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=- 3 y\\qquad\\text{(left side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(2 x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad 3 y,\\ - 3 y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09379f9f-0fae-5fb2-8e11-ae6d471e25dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x y^{2},\\ 2 x y^{2} - x y,\\\\&\\quad x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} + x y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718276} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdbdb8b-52b1-5c66-928f-2cfd0830a765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(2 x y^{2} - x y,\\ - x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad - 2 x y^{2} + x y,\\ x y^{2}\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718290} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6af074-d64a-5243-8aa4-ce846120c1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- 2 x y^{2} + x y,\\ - x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad 2 x y^{2} - x y,\\ x y^{2}\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718291} {:answer/id #uuid "aad01190-e431-5867-92db-d2bf5790a603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} + x y,\\\\&\\quad - x y^{2},\\ 2 x y^{2} - x y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718293} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ceae95-3ba8-5b06-bed5-126f068141c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- 2 x y^{2} + x y,\\ x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad 2 x y^{2} - x y,\\ - x y^{2}\\bigr)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718276} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4bd4f39-0be5-57d2-b58b-988ad97a70fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bef51064-232e-5974-a105-5043f261a2ba" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(x y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x y^{2} - x y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_3}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_1}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_4}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=- x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(left side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=2 x y^{2} - x y\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(right side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=- 2 x y^{2} + x y\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x y^{2},\\ 2 x y^{2} - x y,\\\\&\\quad x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} + x y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e649b992-3d85-5964-a29f-5735b3045703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718351} :answer-field/id #uuid "a59c9a74-49b6-57b6-9fbd-343b535f8c64" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "33fc5116-a35b-53cc-a350-0ea15e74eadf" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/c1/c12715040b1be9b8b40c43a35f1659c2f401c42c71806a75059e071f3dc64756.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x + 2)\\,\\mathbf i+(y + 3)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(2)\\,\\mathbf i+(3)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(2)(4)+(3)(4)-(2)(4)-(3)(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $1(2)=2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(2)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(2)(2-(-2))\\\\&=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(8)=32.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=32+0=32$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $4$ | $16$ |\n| Top | $5$ | $20$ |\n| Left | $0$ | $0$ |\n| Bottom | $-1$ | $-4$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $32$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ddd2d7c-6129-5225-bc26-1a3d25e81f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718345} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c52591a-6655-5949-a485-fdf252aad58c" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "60baf236-c8e8-503c-8318-b36cd4e425e3" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/65/6523762415fc82242d906be9839387f66aa41490690f573bffe71cfb07924fd5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(2 x + 2)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y + 5)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(2 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(2)\\,\\mathbf i+(5)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(2)(4)+(5)(4)-(2)(4)-(5)(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $2(2)=4$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(4)(2-(-2))\\\\&=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(16)=64.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=64+0=64$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Top | $9$ | $36$ |\n| Left | $2$ | $8$ |\n| Bottom | $-1$ | $-4$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $64$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e738a3a-e351-572a-b859-2eb7e1d6fcfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718340} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e16a6e-b4a2-52cb-a0a6-8fc1b63007b4" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c4f5234f-3ee8-5a86-9a70-0189e01402c7" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/31/31cefa640f819acfa55e823e6ef9fec59871f4d9cd646099ef8b15d63909e69c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(- 2 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(1 - 2 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/33/33b0d69e4eff448684f5fdabcc31ba2f48e183917878dc9ab72c3cb51e59584b.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(- 2 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 2 y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(0)\\,\\mathbf i+(1)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(0)(2)+(1)(2)-(0)(2)-(1)(2)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=1\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le 1$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $-2(1)=-2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-1}^{1}(-2)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(-2)(1-(-1))\\\\&=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(-4)=-16.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=-16+0=-16$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $-2$ | $-4$ |\n| Top | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| Left | $-2$ | $-4$ |\n| Bottom | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $-16$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd7439fc-f72e-5835-9f79-96c50c3f4f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718348} :answer-field/id #uuid "514f22bc-3f61-550d-87fa-a76b41fdb2c5" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "da16d5f2-af68-5188-8759-ec052c70a621" :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/ea/eaa690b15fb3324ad446c96544ae6aa0d992d40c8d47eaa6ceefbf9d96c1771c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x - 2)\\,\\mathbf i+(y + 3)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(-2)\\,\\mathbf i+(3)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(-2)(4)+(3)(4)-(-2)(4)-(3)(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $1(2)=2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(2)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(2)(2-(-2))\\\\&=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(8)=32.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=32+0=32$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $0$ | $0$ |\n| Top | $5$ | $20$ |\n| Left | $4$ | $16$ |\n| Bottom | $-1$ | $-4$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $32$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb37cfa-1ee5-57fb-893b-d1a20545e2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718433} {:answer/id #uuid "86a2470a-eb18-5a0e-9025-f4ffe7596f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718433} :answer-field/id #uuid "943faa27-6657-5f1a-8b2e-f5e135dbc04b" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154518f6-ffc6-5f0c-ac81-5ec693948961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718426} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8704cf-5cda-5f9d-bc9d-73241166b42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718429} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27244d7-c724-5180-8edd-9ce12e9ccb4d" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718443} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ec8b68-f322-5a01-a5ba-655044f3b06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718430} {:answer/id #uuid "7e42693c-0f95-5afc-a793-6333f060c3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718437} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd466ff2-9a17-50a7-81f1-fc7f589a19e0" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "14a5b8a4-3b53-5b6e-a2b9-59f9eff2b393" :question/problem "![](images/45/45468822eed304a0075a5aba689657fa397bb95cce81af2cf94c873043941629.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$. Let $C$ be the quarter of the unit circle in the first quadrant traversed from $A=(0,1)$ to $B=(1,0)$, and let $-C$ denote the same curve traversed from $B$ to $A$.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The line integral is the total work done by the field along the oriented path. A force component along the motion contributes positive work; a component against the motion contributes negative work; a perpendicular force contributes zero work.\n\nThe field points radially outward. Motion along the circle is tangent to it, so the force is perpendicular to the direction of motion at every point, even though the force itself is nonzero.\n\n![](images/f5/f55460ef335cf7e23862e7e78be8aaba01174aace3ddae50ba43448140d2a117.png)\n\nEvery local contribution to work is zero. Hence $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. The statement that this integral is negative is false; the statement that it is zero is true.\n\nReversing the direction changes the tangent's direction but preserves perpendicularity.\n\n![](images/bf/bffdd0261970f5ef6779684e61e504be66fe924f378f3027d2ff316729498168.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, so the statement that the reversed integral is positive is false.\n\nSelect only $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cd03cb-a801-5d43-899a-9ddfd2e0d12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718431} {:answer/id #uuid "93e3d9d5-9a25-5e75-a039-e060e624975d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718431} :answer-field/id #uuid "287249d7-2cc0-57cb-9381-3b6251f28540" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78341b62-909a-5aec-af44-cbb544879d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718435} {:answer/id #uuid "f1aaf854-9bce-57aa-89d8-de73af421494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718435} :answer-field/id #uuid "389e6302-31f8-506e-8a11-9394825e2723" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718428} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1bc7500-b48c-5d55-8ec9-27156552150e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718444} {:answer/id #uuid "fb16f244-4103-5fce-a10e-e36ad42bc199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718450} :answer-field/id #uuid "6222c7e1-3bfe-5f37-869f-a7adddd86dc4" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acbfcb4a-344a-5aa9-b3ea-0942cdfba233" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c9cbb706a8c7d474db9a845312fb8549839f9018dae60f01fb3f558d7c2c62c.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(1+y)\\mathbf j$. Let $C$ be the upper unit semicircle traversed from $A=(-1,0)$ through $T=(0,1)$ to $B=(1,0)$, and let $-C$ denote the same curve traversed from $B$ to $A$.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The line integral is the total work done by the field along the oriented path. A force component along the motion contributes positive work; a component against the motion contributes negative work; a perpendicular force contributes zero work.\n\nThe force points upward everywhere on the semicircle. From $A$ to $T$ the particle rises, so the field does positive work. From $T$ to $B$ it descends, so the field does negative work.\n\n![](images/24/24d2dc1405f1bffea52f5a00757d7ee1a801bb1b5b7b7605190ef056a387d42f.png)\n\nTo decide the total, pair a small upward displacement on the left half with the reflected downward displacement at the same height on the right half. The vertical displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. Because the force magnitude is $1+y$, the paired forces have equal magnitudes too. Their work contributions cancel. Horizontal displacement contributes no work because the force is vertical.\n\nThis pairing covers the whole semicircle, giving $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. This is cancellation of positive and negative work, rather than perpendicularity throughout the path. Thus the negative-integral statement is false and the zero-integral statement is true.\n\nOn $-C$, the right half is traversed upward and the left half downward; the same pairs still cancel.\n\n![](images/26/26bc03a3cdd95cff5eaaacbf57d5afd2101ecd58c193cc2fa1a229be78cdb40b.png)\n\nEquivalently, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. The statement that the reversed integral is positive is false.\n\nSelect only $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7670142a-0b35-5df8-9173-6f7ef2842702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718434} {:answer/id #uuid "7be4bee4-05e8-5133-bb1e-dfefca155648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718436} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa03598-a901-518f-946c-094c9c54337c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718439} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be54d3-d610-5e39-9012-eca1ec20a6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718445} {:answer/id #uuid "eba80a4d-9537-5f98-84e9-c6b622f8103d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718436} :answer-field/id #uuid "09f53f57-69e8-54f2-a871-f62603c40336" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cbaed1ac-c3d0-55e5-9ebe-2d4259c73436" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a8a91b00aadcac1ff2b5a22d49e2a9b1e4aa77337cf4c02603dc724867242ac.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\mathbf i-x\\mathbf j$. Let $C$ be the upper unit semicircle traversed from $A=(-1,0)$ to $B=(1,0)$, and let $-C$ denote the same curve traversed from $B$ to $A$.\n\nWhich statements are true?\n\nI. $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$\n\nII. $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$\n\nIII. $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral is the total work done by the field along the oriented path. A force component along the motion contributes positive work; a component against the motion contributes negative work; a perpendicular force contributes zero work.\n\nFrom $A$ to $B$ along the upper semicircle, the particle travels clockwise. The force arrows are tangent to the circle and point clockwise, so the force assists the motion throughout the path.\n\n![](images/be/be22e2ebd35e15a5e7c05e7745e47380b4b9fb9020ebd7a6746413a64b1676fc.png)\n\nThus $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$: statement I is true and statement II is false.\n\nOn reversing the path, the field stays fixed but the motion reverses, so the force now opposes the motion throughout.\n\n![](images/75/75830446bd7b22171226b50904233020686186552d6dbcc20d6b4d444a0468dd.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, so statement III is true.\n\nThe correct answer is **I and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "760f07fe-bdab-5dfb-baea-f7f998796bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718958} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc49056-173f-5aa9-a1f2-d13e04347526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718981} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c58f5bc-f704-5e0f-bb7f-5d64d776d3c2" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3969b994-1ea0-572b-b6ba-2e20a10e63e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718951} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf785c9-233e-5672-8b14-8606d1d99d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718956} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d3da8d-fd0c-5087-b0e2-5e9dce556a3c" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a17e2d4-8c85-5655-810e-93332b2bbc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718964} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd39b3c-73a8-51b1-a7ac-d3d0aa8c65c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718964} :answer-field/id #uuid "e072aa38-b320-5d13-a844-a44b152d1e91" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0175e5b0-1840-50f9-977a-c3438c4b8653" :question/problem "![](images/db/db40e0220317d76c618a495952b74be0fb9b69b7fd5aa07e0e7e1db07f6fca3e.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y,- x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Let $C$ be one-half of this ellipse, traversed clockwise from $A=(0,1)$ to $B=(0,-1)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points with the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore positive.\n\n![](images/8d/8dd16a504be78e59976221c87c3ff9366ae1327ab4a2c01611fdfa9291f7c5fd.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/c6/c69ae349d559b26523d311c7e2bfb7b489084d689368b734ee93389f147499aa.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a81b90c6-0857-5342-963d-8ffbf08da9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718972} {:answer/id #uuid "e6756c55-0916-5a8a-88dc-3efb43288ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718972} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c289f8-b12d-5a4a-87bb-61e48fef9af8" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718949} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117fee37-3964-50ba-a618-5a81552105d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718943} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a04b4a-be20-56e6-9a8c-56b20fab0dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718976} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57a3fe2-2ac7-578c-bdbd-6ef34dd9e260" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718969} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f82ccf8-2699-5f72-86c0-6e26c6c8b568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718941} {:answer/id #uuid "b43fcb20-12a4-5106-ac52-d48611f3a3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718941} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c3749e-1013-550f-874a-be82f6fd807d" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1bec6d2f-fb9a-5052-ac57-22225a53081d" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a789e75d4ba819064a3364d76de1d5b0a9ae7890c9592c70301a72e2c51741b.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y,- 4 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be three-quarters of this ellipse, traversed counterclockwise from $A=(-1,0)$ to $B=(0,2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points against the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore negative.\n\n![](images/c6/c64c8ccfa7f272ab00b1ff8bfe2389402a833863766075ba49679fbd5376cbb7.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/1a/1ab43e6773bc938b65ad94064e442956e8af6408aaebc3014b8108a24773047c.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfe79cf4-5d36-591b-a9c1-2229b218c939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718979} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf0479c-c129-52cf-ad0b-396b32f890ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718979} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec3c61f-f62e-5dc3-8f34-9c3cf54b38c5" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718940} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "784c80f6-007a-5c18-99d5-f6684bac2032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718959} {:answer/id #uuid "7d04e75d-2d66-50a3-8979-17427504a027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718960} :answer-field/id #uuid "a859b3d8-a689-57a7-a519-ad6556692af2" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718973} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c2fc709-1e7f-5000-97ea-4a40750ea115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718952} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6fa1ed-c4b9-567e-a087-1f8fb0c2f7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718978} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc58fe1a-b899-53a0-b79b-7efe6d436e0f" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a06dbfc4-a1ba-546f-bd27-122d42540507" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac30aeac69da8e1f91fd538e4a89b090a96175753ad7435f0c22971fc8c3f681.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 9 y,4 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be one-half of this ellipse, traversed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-2)$ to $B=(0,2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points with the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore positive.\n\n![](images/08/0835be72be55a36cf4357cb7b5b82e58308e79d7481902eeac919a1ca64d637f.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4c4f497d0d97e1f37b6a4c291207c2c3dc140646cc840c76556590cd11248651.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276605f1-227f-52aa-ae11-6d5e39d6a931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718948} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e9da3a-59c5-55dd-8ccb-7c9de365c7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718948} :answer-field/id #uuid "51580800-1e12-5fc3-8a6b-6cd7986ce6fa" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0728ec5a-8486-5051-82a8-684f36dca76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718939} {:answer/id #uuid "2546473e-449d-5a84-aa48-76d7866b71eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718939} :answer-field/id #uuid "86598c24-519f-5952-b4cd-4edb332fdf0d" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afd0898-222b-5ea9-9bd6-9a49618b37f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718944} {:answer/id #uuid "20cfd975-79c4-5a1f-9e3f-dd08bb918905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718944} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb7fdf3-b056-513f-9148-137c54082ae7" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab25337e-afe7-57fc-a903-ea3b7755cbe3" :question/problem "![](images/95/9505a6578e13c299c59a790672e0db3420aeeaecee7ec1dc169a6407e464e131.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 9 y,4 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be three-quarters of this ellipse, traversed clockwise from $A=(-3,0)$ to $B=(0,-2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points against the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore negative.\n\n![](images/43/43fc678ed01a8d6bd7e709c4138e9355e957ed0d3a295d767bfb68a5e4a4bd81.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b802348ea9bb3cc286faf1bbabf8c409c4793590ad86c55783071d61238d1519.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10db9263-1905-50a6-9424-538bdaeaf520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718942} {:answer/id #uuid "368efbcb-4482-5e8b-91c2-8fed729f4109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718942} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6abd28-260c-5933-800e-105eecf3caab" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd8d64d-b55b-56ac-ba8c-7aa48c4806fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718954} {:answer/id #uuid "f4411656-8a90-5426-adc8-41f234465b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718954} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6122d80-811f-5be7-b6b0-fe7609885b9e" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718971} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e9b870-249f-5d5b-be93-311940afd063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718962} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ff0e94-2b50-5333-a74f-405dbabe4453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718962} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfaa5e8-585d-568c-b8a4-fa52a9d6c0e7" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7c77f1d-5118-53bc-9cbf-d1302bf95b8d" :question/problem "![](images/14/1446c8eb7bc22596616d757b5f403d9364e1d69147dfd1a58656a38266df16ff.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 4 y,9 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$. Let $C$ be three-quarters of this ellipse, traversed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-3)$ to $B=(-2,0)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points with the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore positive.\n\n![](images/70/7006fa694d06d062ad4b5596745fdea74bdc7f76ec409a8bdc1ac81c78af8a4b.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/53/53c9dd47332ff72759429d5599687efe24ece88c7eebf02c06cfbffe52a24d5c.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1022725d-2487-5aee-b056-70d3658000ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719074} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0dbfaa-8257-5e39-bc5f-5513dfe1b5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719074} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed3a284-fb05-5bf1-a641-bcbc2839cc8c" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "540f2545-c5c3-51bd-86d9-9c95d5e82e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719084} {:answer/id #uuid "7166635f-61a5-5a2f-a910-9d498cd5bbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719084} :answer-field/id #uuid "15676700-c436-51d1-93b0-8d11e3b255f8" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7fe034-ed3a-53b5-8423-b36452283e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719072} {:answer/id #uuid "21b300c9-3427-5081-a2db-54cf95514c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719072} :answer-field/id #uuid "f259f1cb-23a3-5574-8e9d-62f4a792bd90" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0adc9ef2-0ba0-52ea-9787-889ddbbf73b6" :question/problem "![](images/72/72508c4a3efb65116b33e33aabf582ba521f724139985e67130244c2abda1680.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(0,- y - 3\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $y>0$ from $A=(2,0)$ to $B=(-2,0)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points downward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does negative work; on the second half it does positive work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $y=v$, where $v>0$. Their $x$ coordinates are $u$ and $- u$, where $u>0$. Their small $y$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{y,1}-F_{y,2}&=\\left(- v - 3\\right)-\\left(- v - 3\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe paired forces are equal. Equal opposite displacements therefore give equal opposite work. Every pair cancels, giving zero total work. This is cancellation, rather than perpendicularity everywhere.\n\n![](images/1c/1c78101232dbb9ac923334b258fe54359aa0d93003f8ac31da7359b9deeb195e.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/c6/c668801541df26bccc1da55da266e408e71c35b92ccf7cb717e5a6cd0831331e.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63b8217d-2b5a-5f66-a280-f94a8fb28969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719086} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc72169-5d46-59fb-adb5-b05662e804ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719095} :answer-field/id #uuid "39489cc4-8e21-5c8d-85b1-28e0b11c081c" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b1b21c3-7989-5b04-bd16-8ed3adafa1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719093} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7eeee0-10f0-5c57-807e-33263d2195f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719093} :answer-field/id #uuid "79efa077-f19b-555a-a7b7-153dd011b7d7" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "744df7a4-ce8b-527c-8b04-c99f3351956b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719090} {:answer/id #uuid "7821fb70-fa9d-5133-b82b-3244dc4660db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719091} :answer-field/id #uuid "c857ad36-8879-5cfd-bee9-04cace70634c" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6688dd81-905f-55f2-b49d-7299676c5e3e" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d815ef57c70cb5009946ae5a15a6b942a414ea738e558018b8d8ecbcf604dd03.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(x - y + 2,0\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $x>0$ from $A=(0,-1)$ to $B=(0,1)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points rightward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does positive work; on the second half it does negative work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $x=v$, where $v>0$. Their $y$ coordinates are $- u$ and $u$, where $u>0$. Their small $x$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{x,1}-F_{x,2}&=\\left(u + v + 2\\right)-\\left(- u + v + 2\\right) \\\\ &=2 u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first displacement in $x$ is positive. Multiplying it by the displayed force difference gives positive work for every pair. The positive contribution dominates, so the total work has that sign. Equal endpoint coordinates alone would not establish cancellation.\n\n![](images/cd/cd381c45a7e01660b0cb794ff6d1027aad66d47fc3abfd1fa6b8b9fe5c4f4a03.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/c0/c04e8c91f724439e8609217aba632f47c37872edb396dc88ca52686807400e82.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e24726-f130-5f21-b186-eedaacdeb55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719077} {:answer/id #uuid "de754c50-9160-5747-aaa3-92285c3bf948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719104} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d09197b-19ba-52c3-bb88-d87655fb6349" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07f6ce9-c5f3-561c-a015-d523e0747169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719100} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f4ea42-c1e6-578a-b217-55d1244ec1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719100} :answer-field/id #uuid "51fec652-867c-5e10-be6d-9a67b58c23c4" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "539d897a-4be3-50e3-9c84-aba39a78bdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719083} {:answer/id #uuid "f930e27f-efb1-5db5-80f9-1a6083f09f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719083} :answer-field/id #uuid "c736ab83-e7fc-55df-971c-ebb8d0ecaf1b" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6f8a9dd5-1643-5a79-9e97-e032d77c005b" :question/problem "![](images/56/56bf08ccda891beb79635a79b89a9e503d1a828dd4e8746bb9f35bd3216ea3ed.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(0,- y - 2\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $y>0$ from $A=(1,0)$ to $B=(-1,0)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points downward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does negative work; on the second half it does positive work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $y=v$, where $v>0$. Their $x$ coordinates are $u$ and $- u$, where $u>0$. Their small $y$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{y,1}-F_{y,2}&=\\left(- v - 2\\right)-\\left(- v - 2\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe paired forces are equal. Equal opposite displacements therefore give equal opposite work. Every pair cancels, giving zero total work. This is cancellation, rather than perpendicularity everywhere.\n\n![](images/a5/a58e221c447746fbcc6f28cbdb62d692850e85250cd934ff2b03d90e28760a98.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/4e/4e48c79f38105eb8189b96bcd42b82b49f4bb3284dcd4f65c44f0e3e557d5ba9.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d4a080-4014-587c-9c86-98dcf645ea8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719070} {:answer/id #uuid "62aaa2ab-d160-51b4-ae61-92218132df0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719085} :answer-field/id #uuid "403b4722-2ef8-5417-ab26-72fcb08378dc" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adaf797a-af86-5ae9-b081-ce46553b0c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719097} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c85a38-2da3-5430-9b8b-28f4b5c81466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719108} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c5647b1-f157-5d26-865f-2a5249518936" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "845ece00-7ac1-511d-9257-ccf6e8f5d73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719096} {:answer/id #uuid "d99ca6b8-f7e1-5c7a-9c80-f73b64f0cb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719096} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4326e32-338f-5449-b802-5732da669b81" :answer-field/key "$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b0ab9277-c001-5758-bae6-5cf1a9940c0a" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d5e961f47eb897419a1de496c142dee2d4f2bac56547cf9a2eda6ad73f53e07.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x - y + 3,0\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $x<0$ from $A=(0,-2)$ to $B=(0,2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points rightward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does negative work; on the second half it does positive work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $x=- v$, where $v>0$. Their $y$ coordinates are $- u$ and $u$, where $u>0$. Their small $x$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{x,1}-F_{x,2}&=\\left(u + v + 3\\right)-\\left(- u + v + 3\\right) \\\\ &=2 u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first displacement in $x$ is negative. Multiplying it by the displayed force difference gives negative work for every pair. The negative contribution dominates, so the total work has that sign. Equal endpoint coordinates alone would not establish cancellation.\n\n![](images/dc/dcbe02c44ca96053446a8e9d223f9f0aaff6fb2f142406c10bbeb67042b44f3e.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/8c/8cef4d6e4825021b497faa489b483465877a68792e5770dd22aba7ffa0433e42.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**."}]}
